def render_story(icon):
    return f'''<article class="edition-story-page" lang="en"><header class="story-page-opening"><div><p class="eyebrow">Our story</p><h1>A father’s poems.<br>A son’s care.</h1><p class="story-page-deck">Giving <i>Singhasan</i> a home where another generation can read, listen and return.</p></div><img src="assets/book-ornaments/poets-notebook-v1.webp" alt="" width="1254" height="1254"></header><div class="opening-waterline" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="story-page-body"><section aria-labelledby="story-book"><h2 id="story-book">The book at the heart of it.</h2><p><i>Singhasan</i> is Pravakar Satapathy’s first book of Odia poems, written during his involvement in politics. Across sixty-eight poems, he draws connections between power and ordinary life, mythology and the world around him.</p><p>This online edition grew from his son Ahimanikya’s wish to bring those poems to more readers. The original Odia remains at its centre. Illustrations, a quiet reading option and translations offer different ways to spend time with the same book.</p><a class="story-page-link" href="author.html">Meet the poet <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section><section aria-labelledby="story-translation"><p class="eyebrow">Across languages</p><h2 id="story-translation">Let the feeling travel.</h2><p>A poem carries more than the literal meaning of its words. Its humour, cultural associations, rhythm and emotional turns matter too. Our translation work follows that thought: help a reader in another language feel what the poem is doing, while staying close to its Odia source.</p><a class="story-page-link" href="{TRANSLATION_ROUTE}">{icon('translate')}<span>Read about translation</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section><section aria-labelledby="story-borders"><p class="eyebrow">A wider home for poetry</p><h2 id="story-borders">Poem Without Borders</h2><p><i>Singhasan</i> is part of Ahimanikya’s wider creative project, Poem Without Borders—a place for poetry to find readers through languages, images, music and conversation.</p><div class="story-page-links"><a class="story-page-link" href="https://kabitawithoutborders.org/">Explore Poem Without Borders <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="story-page-link" href="https://kabitawithoutborders.org/#story">Read how it began <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></section><section class="story-colophon" aria-labelledby="story-credit"><p class="eyebrow">The care behind these pages</p><h2 id="story-credit">Reimagined with care.</h2><p>This online edition was reimagined and designed by <a href="https://kabitawithoutborders.org/#story">Ahimanikya Satapathy</a>, the poet’s son, who shaped its design system, artistic direction and reading experience.</p><p>The poems are by Pravakar Satapathy.</p></section><a class="story-page-link" href="contents.html">Find a poem <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article>'''
